# Environment variables, as defined in CLAUDE.md.
# Copy to .env for local use. Never commit a filled-in copy: this file exists so that
# secrets stay out of the repository (global CLAUDE.md §8).

# --- LLM: two providers, one client contract ---------------------------------
# `openai` is an OpenAI-compatible endpoint - NIM in development, any compatible API in
# production - and `bedrock` is Amazon Bedrock's Converse API against Amazon Nova
# (docs/adr/0022-*.md). One is live per process and there is no fallback between them.
#
# **`openai` is the default and local development keeps it**, so everything below behaves as
# it always has. The AWS deployment sets `bedrock`, where there is no endpoint and no key at
# all: the ECS worker task role carries `bedrock:InvokeModel` and boto3 signs with it.
#
# Swapping a model is a config change plus scripts/check_model.py plus an eval run - never a
# code change.
LLM_PROVIDER=openai

# --- LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock: two values, no credential -------------------------
# Read only when LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock, and then LLM_BASE_URL / LLM_API_KEY / LLM_MODEL are
# not read at all. BEDROCK_MODEL_ID takes either a foundation model id
# (`amazon.nova-pro-v1:0`) or a cross-region inference profile id
# (`apac.amazon.nova-pro-v1:0`) - Converse takes both in the same field. BEDROCK_REGION falls
# back to AWS_REGION.
#
# **There is deliberately no BEDROCK_API_KEY**, and nothing here holds an AWS access key:
# boto3's ordinary credential chain finds the task role, exactly as it does for SQS and S3.
BEDROCK_MODEL_ID=
BEDROCK_REGION=

# --- LLM_PROVIDER=openai: the endpoint, the models, the key ------------------
# WORKER ONLY, since docs/adr/0012-*.md. The API process starts, serves every route and
# passes its health check with none of the four below set, and with no TAVILY_API_KEY - it
# writes a row and enqueues a pointer, and never calls a model or fetches a page. The
# processes that do assume an LLM refuse to start without them instead: `python -m worker`,
# scripts/check_model.py, and scripts/measure_jobs.py.
LLM_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1
LLM_MODEL=
# Cheap model for Supervisor routing and reflection scoring only.
# Falls back to LLM_MODEL when unset.
LLM_FAST_MODEL=
LLM_API_KEY=
# Shared client-side rate limit. The NIM free tier allows roughly 40 requests/minute.
LLM_RPM_LIMIT=40
# How long one main-model request may take. engineering-guidelines.md §17 sets 60s and that
# is the default; raise it only where the endpoint is slow. The NIM development tier
# generates at roughly 15-20 output tokens/second, which turns 60s into a cap of about a
# thousand output tokens - under a full report, so development uses 120. The fast tier is
# fixed at 30s: its callers receive a handful of tokens.
LLM_MAIN_TIMEOUT_S=60

# --- Web search --------------------------------------------------------------
# Independent of LLM_PROVIDER: the research tools need this whichever model answers.
TAVILY_API_KEY=

# --- Data stores --------------------------------------------------------------
# The ordinary libpq connection string - postgresql://user:password@host:5432/research -
# which is what psql, RDS, and the checkpointer's own pool all take. The SQLAlchemy driver
# name is added in code (database/queries.py), so it does not belong here.
#
# Used by `alembic upgrade head`, by the Postgres checkpointer, and by the audit-trail
# writes (implementation steps 13-16). Nothing reaches for it on its own: a process that
# wants durability passes it in, so leaving it unset runs the graph exactly as Phase 1 did
# rather than pointing at a server that is not running.
DATABASE_URL=

# The same URL in five parts, read ONLY when DATABASE_URL is unset (docs/adr/0020-*.md
# decision 1). They exist for AWS: RDS generates and holds its own master password in a
# secret it owns - which is what keeps the password out of terraform.tfstate - and that
# secret carries {username, password} with no host, so nothing upstream can compose a URL.
# The container does it instead. All four of HOST, NAME, USER and PASSWORD must be present
# or the answer is "no database configured", exactly as an unset DATABASE_URL is.
#
# Leave them empty locally. DATABASE_URL always wins.
DB_HOST=
DB_PORT=
DB_NAME=
DB_USER=
DB_PASSWORD=
# Short-term state, and **worker-required since Phase 3 stage 3** (step 21). It holds the two
# tool caches, the per-job URL set, and the shared LLM rate limiter - and the last of those is
# why the worker refuses to start when Redis does not answer: the limiter fails closed, so
# without it no LLM call can be made at all (engineering-guidelines.md §11, §17).
#
# The API reads it for one thing only: `/health` reports `checks.redis`, which is how a
# deployment finds out its workers cannot work.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# --- AWS: required from Phase 3 ----------------------------------------------
# The job queue. **Required by both the API and the worker** since Phase 3 stage 2: the API
# enqueues one pointer message per submitted job and per reviewer decision, and the worker
# is what receives them. It must be a FIFO queue with a redrive policy - the worker reads
# both at startup and refuses to run without them (docs/adr/0010-*.md decisions 4 and 8).
#
# Against the local Compose stack:
#   SQS_QUEUE_URL=http://localhost:4566/000000000000/research-jobs.fifo
SQS_QUEUE_URL=
S3_BUCKET=
AWS_REGION=ap-south-1
# Where the AWS APIs live when they are not AWS. Unset against real AWS; the LocalStack
# address locally. It is the only difference between the two, which is what makes the
# `integration`-marked tests worth running - the same client, the same calls, a different
# address. Credentials still have to be present for boto3 to sign a request; LocalStack
# ignores their values, so AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test will do.
#   AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:4566
AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=
# Where a client OUTSIDE the deployment reaches the same S3, when that is a different address.
# `GET /jobs/{id}/report` answers a presigned URL, and a presigned URL is a signed *address*:
# SigV4 covers the host, so it cannot be rewritten afterwards without breaking the signature.
#
# Unset against real AWS - the bucket's address is the same from both sides. It exists for the
# local stack, where they genuinely differ: the worker writes through `localstack:4566` on the
# Compose network and a browser on this machine can only reach `localhost:4566`. Read by the
# API only; the worker and the re-export script use AWS_ENDPOINT_URL, which is where a real
# PutObject belongs.
#   S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:4566
S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_URL=

# --- Observability -----------------------------------------------------------
LANGSMITH_TRACING=false
# Required when LANGSMITH_TRACING is true.
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=
LANGSMITH_PROJECT=competitive-research

# --- Limits: the loop guards and content caps --------------------------------
# Two automatic improvement cycles, so at most three report-producing passes.
MAX_REVISIONS=2
# Routing oscillation guard. 20 is the automatic workflow's derived maximum (5 subtopics
# + 2 revisions, engineering-guidelines.md §5). A reviewer edit costs +1 legitimate hop, so
# bounding edits at 3 (docs/adr/0006-*.md, accepted) puts the ceiling at 23 and 24 stays
# correct as the ceiling plus one - it is NOT reduced to 20. Development overrides this to 30.
MAX_SUPERVISOR_HOPS=24
MAX_LLM_CALLS_PER_JOB=60
# How many times a reviewer may send one job back for an edit (docs/adr/0006-*.md). An edit
# is one Synthesizer pass over the evidence the job already has - never new research - and
# costs 3 calls and 1 hop, which is what puts the hop ceiling at 23.
MAX_REVIEWER_EDITS=3
# The no-new-node deadline for ONE WORKER INVOCATION, in seconds - not a hard wall and not a
# job's lifetime (docs/adr/0010-*.md decision 7, clarified by ADR 0015). Checked after each
# durable checkpoint: once reached, the worker starts no further node and records
# `failure_reason="job_timeout"`; a node already running may finish after it. SQS ownership is
# maintained independently by visibility renewal.
MAX_JOB_RUNTIME=1200
REFLECTION_PASS_THRESHOLD=3.5
MAX_FETCH_BYTES=2097152
MAX_PAGE_CHARS=24000
# How many of one subtopic's page extractions run at once (docs/adr/0002-*.md). Choosing and
# fetching sources stays sequential; only the extraction calls overlap. Refused outside 1-3 at
# startup rather than clamped. Set to 1 to restore strictly sequential extraction.
#
# RATE LIMIT: this bounds how many requests ONE JOB holds open at once. What bounds the
# deployment is the shared Redis limiter, built at step 21: LLM_RPM_LIMIT is now one window
# across every worker rather than a ceiling each process keeps to itself. One job measured
# 1.8 req/min against LLM_RPM_LIMIT=40; at 3 it is roughly 5.4 (engineering-guidelines.md §13).
RESEARCHER_CONCURRENCY=3

# --- Auth: required from Phase 2 ---------------------------------------------
# WHICH credential the API accepts, and only one is live at a time (docs/adr/0020-*.md).
#   api_key  the Phase 2 hashed key table below. The DEFAULT, and what every local command,
#            the Compose stack and the whole offline suite use.
#   cognito  a Cognito access token, verified against the pool's published signing keys. The
#            AWS deployment's mode, and then the three COGNITO_* values below are required
#            and AUTH_KEYS is not read at all.
# An API that accepted either would be exactly as strong as the weaker of the two.
AUTH_MODE=api_key

# Read only when AUTH_MODE=cognito. NONE OF THE THREE IS A SECRET: verifying a token needs a
# published key, a published issuer and a client id, which is why they are plain variables
# here while LLM_API_KEY is not. COGNITO_REGION falls back to AWS_REGION.
COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=
COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=
COGNITO_REGION=

# The API keys, HASHED, as JSON - never the keys themselves:
#   {"<sha256 hex of the key>": {"user_id": "<uuid>", "role": "reviewer"}}
# Two roles: `submitter` submits and reads its own jobs; `reviewer` may also decide at the
# gate. Print a digest with:
#   python -c "import hashlib,sys;print(hashlib.sha256(sys.argv[1].encode()).hexdigest())" KEY
AUTH_KEYS=
# Secrets Manager id holding that same JSON. Read from Phase 5; unused before then.
AUTH_KEYS_SECRET_ID=

# --- Operations --------------------------------------------------------------
# How old a queued or running job must be before `scripts/reconcile_jobs.py` will look at it
# (docs/adr/0021-*.md decision 2). It chooses which rows are INSPECTED and authorises nothing:
# every mutation still needs the per-job PostgreSQL execution fence, a fresh reread of durable
# state, and outcome-specific evidence. No job is ever failed for being old.
#
# 7200 is derived: a message can spend three deliveries of the queue's 1800s visibility window
# in flight before it is dead-lettered (5400s), and 7200 is that plus half an hour.
STALE_JOB_MIN_AGE_SECONDS=7200
RETENTION_DAYS=365
APP_ENV=local
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- Local infrastructure: NOT read by config.py ------------------------------
# Everything above is CLAUDE.md's environment table. The three below are read by
# `docker compose` and by the test suite, and never by the application.

# Docker Compose reads this file for its own interpolation. Set POSTGRES_PORT when 5432 is
# already taken on your machine - another project's container is enough - and follow it in
# DATABASE_URL and TEST_DATABASE_URL. REDIS_PORT and LOCALSTACK_PORT do the same for 6379
# and 4566, and API_PORT for the containerised API's 8000.
#
# These move the HOST port only. Inside the Compose network every service is reached by its
# name on its real port - postgres:5432, redis:6379, localstack:4566 - so changing one of
# these never changes a container's configuration.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
API_PORT=8000

# --- The application containers (step 22b/c) ----------------------------------
# `docker compose up -d --wait` still starts infrastructure only. The API and the worker are
# behind the `app` profile, so they are opted into:
#   docker compose --profile app build
#   docker compose --profile app up -d --wait
#
# The API needs AUTH_KEYS to start. docker-compose.yml carries a LOCAL DEVELOPMENT table so a
# fresh checkout can call it, and these are the plaintext keys behind those two hashes:
#   submitter: local-development-submitter-key
#   reviewer:  local-development-reviewer-key
# They are published deliberately, which is exactly why they must never reach a deployment -
# set AUTH_KEYS here or in the environment to replace the table. In AWS it comes from Secrets
# Manager (engineering-guidelines.md §16).
#
# The worker's four provider variables are PASSED THROUGH from this file rather than defaulted
# in docker-compose.yml, because a compose file in a repository is not a place a credential may
# have a value. Unset, `python -m worker` refuses to start and names the first one missing.

# The database the `postgres`-marked tests run against:
#   docker compose up -d --wait
#   TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://research:research@localhost:5432/research_test pytest -m postgres
# Every case drops its schema first, so the harness refuses a URL whose database name does
# not contain `test`. Compose creates research_test beside research for exactly this.
# It is read from the process environment, never from this file: pytest does not load .env,
# and that is deliberate - a URL sitting in a file must not turn the offline suite into one
# that opens sockets.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# The Redis database the `redis`-marked tests run against:
#   docker compose up -d --wait
#   TEST_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/15 pytest -m redis
# Every case flushes the database it is given, so the harness REFUSES database 0 - which is what
# REDIS_URL defaults to, and therefore the one an application run locally would be using. Read
# from the process environment, never from this file.
TEST_REDIS_URL=

# The LocalStack endpoint the `integration`-marked tests use, on exactly the same terms:
#   docker compose up -d --wait
#   SQS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:4566 pytest -m integration
# They read the queue Compose declares, create throwaway queues of their own, and drive the
# real worker against a real message. Unset, every one of them skips, which is what keeps
# plain `pytest` offline. Read from the process environment, never from this file.
SQS_ENDPOINT_URL=

# The fourth marked layer, and the only one that needs the application image rather than a
# service. It drives the containers the `app` profile starts, so they have to be up first -
# and started with credentials that cannot reach a real provider, which the first test in
# tests/test_container_runtime.py refuses to run without:
#   docker compose --profile app build
#   export LLM_BASE_URL=https://llm.invalid/v1 LLM_MODEL=test-model
#   export LLM_API_KEY=test TAVILY_API_KEY=test
#   docker compose --profile app up -d --wait
#   TEST_CONTAINER_STACK=1 pytest -m container
# Every line above is a complete command on its own. A shell continuation would not survive
# being written as a comment: the `#` starting the next line lands inside the command and
# truncates it silently. The four values are fake and local-only - `.invalid` cannot resolve.
# Read from the process environment, never from this file.
TEST_CONTAINER_STACK=
